---
title: Annotations
description: Map, validate, transform, and warn about constructor fields.
---

Annotations are in `net.ririfa.yacla.annotation`. Apply them to primary constructor parameters.

| Annotation | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `@Key("name")` | Prefers the supplied document key |
| `@Range(min, max)` | Checks inclusive numeric bounds when a value is present |
| `@NotBlank` | Rejects present blank strings; null is not rejected by this annotation alone |
| `@BlankToNull` | Turns blank strings into null |
| `@SetOf` | Converts a collection to a set; an empty set becomes null |
| `@EnumList` / `@EnumSet` | Supported markers; enum collections also convert without them |
| `@Loader(MyLoader::class)` | Transforms a non-null raw value before built-in conversion |
| `@Warn("message")` | Logs when the processed value is null, if a logger is configured |

## Validate fields

```kotlin
import net.ririfa.yacla.annotation.NotBlank
import net.ririfa.yacla.annotation.Range

data class Credentials(
    @NotBlank val token: String,
    @Range(min = 1, max = 60) val timeoutSeconds: Int = 10
)
```

`token` is required because its type is non-null and it has no default. `@NotBlank` rejects whitespace-only strings. For a nullable token, use nullability and defaults deliberately; `@NotBlank` alone does not make it required.

Annotations validate supplied values during mapping. Omitted constructor defaults are not separately revalidated.

## Custom field loader

```kotlin
import net.ririfa.yacla.annotation.Loader
import net.ririfa.yacla.loader.FieldLoader

class TrimLoader : FieldLoader {
    override fun load(raw: Any?): Any? = raw?.toString()?.trim()
}

data class NamedConfig(
    @Loader(TrimLoader::class) val name: String? = null
)
```

Custom loaders must be constructible by Kotlin reflection, for example with a no-argument constructor. Missing or null raw values do not invoke the custom loader.

## Processing order

Yacla applies the custom loader, built-in conversion, `@BlankToNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Range`, `@SetOf`, and finally `@Warn`. Constructor defaults and nullability are resolved afterward. A warning can therefore be emitted before a constructor default is selected.
